---
// Deep Dive: HERA dissected. A narrative of decisions, not a feature tour.
// Claims follow YC-FOUNDER-EVIDENCE-LEDGER: nothing unverified is stated as current.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MediaFrame from '../../components/MediaFrame.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../data/site';
import { heraMedia } from '../../data/lineage';

const shifts = [
  ['Create a project before you can begin', 'start with the messy thought and let structure emerge'],
  ['Translate the idea into a setup form', 'talk naturally while HERA turns the conversation into an Objective'],
  ['Accept a plan that becomes outdated', 'use a living roadmap that changes when real work is accepted'],
  ['Manage one long list of tasks', 'run clear task lanes in sequence or in parallel'],
  ['Lose useful work inside a chat', 'save each result as a versioned artifact the user can review'],
  ['Trust an AI-generated activity log', 'show “Worked for” only when real runtime events support it'],
  ['Open a different modal for every decision', 'use one composer that changes at the right moment'],
];

const workingToday = [
  'Start with a real AI conversation in the Void',
  'Move a proven idea into a room without losing the conversation',
  'Set an Objective, generate a roadmap, and accept the plan',
  'Run tasks that produce durable, versioned artifacts',
  'Approve work, request changes, or return to an earlier version',
  'Advance tasks, goals, and the roadmap from accepted work',
  'Show “Worked for” only when the runtime provides real evidence',
];

const inProgress = [
  'Giving every run reliable access to the right room context',
  'Improving room naming and Objective interpretation',
  'Combining the remaining review paths into one clear experience',
  'Replanning the roadmap when completed work changes what should happen next',
];

const notYet = ['Public users; moderated founder tests are being scheduled', 'macOS build', 'Released binaries'];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Deep Dive"
  description="How HERA turns a messy idea into coordinated, reviewable work without losing the founder’s intent along the way."
>
  <!-- The observation -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Deep Dive</p>
      <h1 class="mt-6 text-h1 font-semibold tracking-tight">From a messy thought to finished work<br /><span class="font-normal">without losing the idea along the way.</span></h1>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-8">
        <p>
          AI can now generate pieces of a product faster than a founder can keep the <em>whole product coherent</em>. I
          learned this while directing coding agents to build HERA. Writing code was no longer the hardest part.
          Preserving the decisions, context, handoffs, and quality around that code was.
        </p>
        <p>
          Chat tools organize conversations. Project tools assume the plan already exists. <br>
          <strong>HERA begins with the conversation, works out what the person is trying to make, and carries that
          intent through planning, execution, and review.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The first mistake -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">The first version made the user do HERA’s job</h2>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          The first HERA asked people to create rooms, complete setup forms, and organize their own work before the
          AI could help. It looked different from ordinary project software, but it repeated the same mistake:
          <strong>the human still had to translate a half-formed idea into the system’s structure.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          That was backwards. A person should be allowed to arrive with an unfinished thought. HERA should do the
          work of understanding what that thought needs to become.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="container-page mt-10 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
        heraMedia.oldVersion.map((shot) => (
          <MediaFrame image={shot.image} caption={shot.caption} class="reveal" />
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The shifts -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">What changed</h2>
      <p class="prose-hera mt-6">
        HERA now follows <strong>one rule:</strong> the system should take responsibility for turning a person’s intent into the
        right structure, state, and next action. The user should not have to become the project manager for the AI.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 space-y-0 rounded-card border border-line bg-surface">
        {
          shifts.map(([before, after], i) => (
            <div
              class:list={['flex flex-col gap-1 px-5 py-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:gap-4', i > 0 && 'border-t border-line']}
            >
              <span class="text-sm text-faint line-through decoration-faint/60 sm:w-56 sm:shrink-0">{before}</span>
              <span class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">{after}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Discovery 1: The Void -->
  <section id="void" class="section border-t border-line scroll-mt-24">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">The Void <br> begin before you know what the idea is</h2>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          Most software asks you to classify a thought before you have finished thinking it. Is this a project, a
          task, a note, or just a conversation? At the beginning, you often do not know.
        </p>
        <p>
          In HERA, you begin in the Void and talk naturally. When the idea becomes clear enough to pursue, HERA
          moves it into a room and preserves the conversation that produced it. The user does not start over, copy
          context into another tool, or rebuild the idea from memory.
          <strong>The thought earns its structure instead of being forced into one.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The conversation and migration path work today. Room naming and Objective interpretation are still being
          improved, so the transition can become as natural as the idea itself.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MediaFrame missing={heraMedia.migrationRecording.missing} class="reveal mt-8" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Discovery 2: The composer -->
  <section id="composer" class="section border-t border-line scroll-mt-24">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">The Composer <br> one place for the decision that matters now</h2>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          The conversation shows what happened. The workspace holds the latest accepted version of the work. The
          composer handles whatever is <em>still unresolved</em>. That may be a normal message, a structured Survey, a
          permission request, a task form, or a review decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          HERA does not open a new modal every time the workflow changes. The same composer transforms, the
          transcript stays visible, and the user can understand why the work paused and what will happen after they
          respond.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MediaFrame missing={heraMedia.composerStrip.missing} class="reveal mt-8" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Discovery 3: The living roadmap -->
  <section id="roadmap" class="section border-t border-line scroll-mt-24">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">The Living Roadmap <br> The plan moves as work progresses</h2>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          Most AI planning tools generate a plan and leave the user to maintain it. HERA connects the plan to the
          work itself. When a task produces a result and that result is accepted, the task advances, its goal
          advances, and the next eligible work can begin.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tasks can run in sequence or in parallel. Later work stays locked until the work it depends on is truly
          finished. The founder does not have to update a separate checklist just to tell the system what already
          happened.
        </p>
        <p>
          I use this same workflow to build HERA. The product keeps the plan, the execution, the evidence, and the
          review connected while the product changes underneath me.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MediaFrame missing={heraMedia.roadmapRecording.missing} class="reveal mt-8" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Honesty as architecture -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">HERA should never pretend work happened</h2>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6">
        <p>
          AI products often narrate progress they cannot prove. A model says it searched, tested, fixed, or finished
          something, and the interface repeats the claim as fact.
        </p>
        <p>
          HERA treats the runtime as the source of truth. “Worked for” appears only when real events support it.
          Deliberation appears only when the provider actually returned it. When there is no evidence, HERA stays
          silent instead of manufacturing activity. <br>
          <strong>The user should be able to trust the difference between work attempted, work completed, and work
          accepted.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
      <MediaFrame missing={heraMedia.workedForShot.missing} class="reveal mt-8 aspect-[2/1]" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Current truth -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">What a user can do today</h2>
      <p class="prose-hera mt-6">
        HERA is a working local MVP. These are the parts that work now, the foundations I am strengthening, and the
        parts that have not shipped yet.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Working now</p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            {workingToday.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Being strengthened</p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            {inProgress.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Not yet</p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            {notYet.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why me -->
  <section class="section border-t border-line">
    <div class="container-prose reveal">
      <div class="prose-hera">
        <p>
          HERA is the result of a pattern I have followed for years. At Ubisoft, I redesign complex internal systems
          when the software has placed too much work, memory, or interpretation on the user. Outside work, I have
          built more than sixteen tools around focus, context, behaviour, and execution. HERA brings those lessons
          together in one product built for people trying to make something real with AI.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a
        href={withBase('work/lineage/')}
        class="mt-6 inline-block text-sm text-ink underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink"
      >
        See the systems that led here →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
